



Step-by-Step SSH Setup (Complete Guide)
1. Generate SSH Key Pair
bash# Generate a new SSH key
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "your.email@gmail.com"

# Prompts you'll see:
# - File location: Press Enter (uses default ~/.ssh/id_ed25519)
# - Passphrase: Press Enter twice (no passphrase) or type one for extra security
What this creates:

~/.ssh/id_ed25519 - Private key (NEVER share this)
~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub - Public key (safe to share)

2. Set Correct Permissions
bash# SSH requires strict permissions for security
chmod 700 ~/.ssh                    # Directory: only you can access
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519         # Private key: only you can read
chmod 644 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub     # Public key: you read/write, others read
3. Add Key to SSH Agent
bash# Start the SSH agent
eval "$(ssh-agent -s)"

# Add your private key to the agent
ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
Why? The agent holds your key in memory so you don't need to enter your passphrase repeatedly.
4. Copy Public Key
bash# Display your public key
cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub

# Or copy directly to clipboard (macOS)
cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub | pbcopy
5. Add Public Key to GitHub

Go to https://github.com/settings/keys
Click "New SSH key"
Title: "MacBook Personal" (or any descriptive name)
Key type: Authentication Key
Key: Paste your public key
Click "Add SSH key"

6. Test SSH Connection
bash# Test authentication
ssh -T git@github.com

# Expected output:
# Hi username! You've successfully authenticated, but GitHub does not provide shell access.
7. Configure Repository to Use SSH
bash# Check current remote URL
git remote -v

# If it shows HTTPS (https://github.com/...), change to SSH:
git remote set-url origin git@github.com:username/repo-name.git

# Verify the change
git remote -v
8. Push Your Code
bash# Now you can push without credential prompts
git push origin main
For Multiple GitHub Accounts
If you have work and personal accounts:
bash# Create SSH config file
nano ~/.ssh/config
```

Add:
```
# Personal GitHub
Host github.com
    HostName github.com
    User git
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
    IdentitiesOnly yes

# Work GitHub
Host github-work
    HostName github.com
    User git
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_work
    IdentitiesOnly yes
Then use different remotes:
bash# Personal repos
git remote set-url origin git@github.com:princythomas98/repo.git

# Work repos
git remote set-url origin git@github-work:princythomas-angelone/repo.git
Quick Reference: First Time Push
bash# 1. Initialize repo
git init

# 2. Add files
git add .

# 3. Commit
git commit -m "Initial commit"

# 4. Add remote (SSH)
git remote add origin git@github.com:username/repo-name.git

# 5. Push
git push -u origin main
# -u flag sets upstream, so future pushes just need: git push
Why SSH Failed Initially
Your error was "Permissions 0777 too open" because:

chmod 777 = Everyone can read/write/execute
SSH requires 600 (only you can read/write) for security
If others can read your private key, they can impersonate you
